Take the same two halves and pair every point with its reflection. The seam becomes a whole ladder of rungs.

Let L=\beth_\omega+1. For each x\in L, connect x to its reflected copy on the other strand:

\text{strand }L
\qquad x\;\longleftrightarrow\;x^{\mathrm{rev}}
\qquad \text{strand }L^{\mathrm{rev}}

Twist that ladder and you get your double helix. If you follow one strand in its forward order, every rung you pass meets the other strand in reverse order. Even your original +1 gets a rung. It pairs the last point of one strand with the first point of the other.

We started by asking whose infinity was bigger. Now your mirror gives us something size alone can’t tell us: which points are tied together.